% ============================================================================
% checks-and-balances — which act each part of the U.S. federal government
% can take against the others (adj-facts-stdlib, CIVICS).
% ============================================================================
% The THIRD library in the `civics/` domain, and the first one here that is
% genuinely RELATIONAL rather than a lookup: its rows are not "X is a kind
% of Y" but "actor A can do action B to object C". Recall is a binding
% query:
%
%     ? checks_and_balances(president, $A, $O)
%       % veto      legislation_created_by_congress
%       % nominate  federal_agency_heads_and_high_court_appointees
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5) with THREE columns — the same arity
% `physics/energy-conversion-example.adj` already ships — so each `row`
% lowers to a ground relation `checks_and_balances(actor, action, object)`
% carrying the source citation. The lookup above is the ordinary SLD
% binding query: the engine returns the act WITH its source, on the CPU,
% with zero model calls, and abstains on any act not one of these five
% source-cited rows. Every column can be the bound one, which is what
% makes this table answer questions the two sibling civics libraries
% cannot:
%
%     ? checks_and_balances($A, veto, $O)          % who can veto?  -> president
%     ? checks_and_balances($A, $Act, president)   % who acts on the president?
%       % congress  remove_from_office
%
% ROW SHAPE — each row is a subject-verb-object decomposition of exactly
% one `<li>` in the page's "How each branch of government provides checks
% and balances" list. Nothing is merged across bullets, and nothing the
% source states as a single act is split:
%
%     actor          action              object
%     -------------  ------------------  ----------------------------------------------
%     president      veto                legislation_created_by_congress
%     president      nominate            federal_agency_heads_and_high_court_appointees
%     congress       confirm_or_reject   presidential_nominees
%     congress       remove_from_office  president
%     supreme_court  overturn            unconstitutional_laws
%
% Each row's own grounding sentence is carried verbatim in the `source`/
% `cites` tail below, so every row is auditable back to one sentence.
%
% WHY THE THIRD COLUMN IS THE OBJECT ACTED UPON, NOT THE BRANCH ACTED
% UPON. The obvious design for a checks-and-balances table is
% branch -> action -> branch, and it is WRONG for this source. Three of
% the five sentences do name another part of government as their object
% (Congress, the president's nominees, the president), but the Supreme
% Court's does not: it names LAWS. Typing the column as a branch would
% force one of two bad outcomes — abstain on the Supreme Court row, which
% leaves the JUDICIAL branch unrepresented in a table whose whole subject
% is the balance BETWEEN the three branches; or infer "unconstitutional
% laws" -> "Congress", which this sentence never states (it does not say
% whose laws, and courts also review state law and executive action).
% Typing the column as the OBJECT ACTED UPON keeps all three branches
% represented, with every row verbatim-grounded and nothing inferred. A
% learner who wants the branch-to-branch graph can still get it where the
% source actually supports it, by composing this table with the sibling
% `government-branch-member.adj` — the same composition-over-assertion
% principle `congress-chamber.adj` was split out for.
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from USA.gov's "Branches of the U.S.
% government" page, "How each branch of government provides checks and
% balances" section. The raw markup was curl-fetched and read
% byte-for-byte before writing this file (not just an AI-summarized
% WebFetch) — confirmed the section is an `<h2>` followed by a lead-in
% `<p>`, a second `<p>` reading "Each branch of government can change acts
% of the other branches:", and a `<ul>` of exactly three `<li>` items,
% which between them state the five acts tabled above. `trust
% authoritative` — USA.gov is the U.S. General Services Administration's
% official guide to government information, a first-party .gov publisher
% of exactly the fact being recalled, the same tier and the SAME page the
% two sibling civics libraries already cite.
%
% Honest abstention:
%
%   - "These justices are nominated by the president and confirmed by the
%     Senate." — a real sentence in the same `<li>`, but a PASSIVE
%     restatement of acts this table already holds in the active voice
%     (`president nominate ...` and `congress confirm_or_reject ...`).
%     Tabling it again would double-count the same two checks under
%     swapped subjects, so a recall on a `justices`-shaped actor abstains.
%   - "The ability of each branch to respond to the actions of the other
%     branches is the system of checks and balances." — the section's
%     lead-in. It DEFINES the system; it is not an act by a branch, so it
%     has no actor/action/object to decompose into a row.
%   - Any act not among these five — including every power named in the
%     page's separate per-branch "key roles" lists (drafting proposed
%     laws, surveying the budget, declaring war, ...), which describe what
%     a branch does in its OWN right rather than what it does to ANOTHER
%     branch — is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS rather than
%     inventing a check.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table checks_and_balances {
    columns actor, action, object

    row (president, veto, legislation_created_by_congress)
    row (president, nominate, federal_agency_heads_and_high_court_appointees)
    row (congress, confirm_or_reject, presidential_nominees)
    row (congress, remove_from_office, president)
    row (supreme_court, overturn, unconstitutional_laws)

    source "The president can veto legislation created by Congress."
    locator "https://www.usa.gov/branches-of-government"
    trust authoritative

    cites "He or she also nominates heads of federal agencies and high court appointees." locator "https://www.usa.gov/branches-of-government"
    cites "Congress confirms or rejects the president's nominees." locator "https://www.usa.gov/branches-of-government"
    cites "It can also remove the president from office in exceptional circumstances." locator "https://www.usa.gov/branches-of-government"
    cites "The Justices of the Supreme Court can overturn unconstitutional laws." locator "https://www.usa.gov/branches-of-government"
}
